// Feature fixture: a dead dependency chain deeper than the optimizer's
// fixpoint round cap (`optimizer._MAX_ROUNDS`), which is the one input shape
// that makes the optimizer stop with work still left to do.
//
// Dead store elimination computes its live-name set once per round, so it can
// only peel the outermost link off a chain of dead stores per pass: removing
// `... -> a14` is what makes `a13` dead, and that is not noticed until the
// next round. A chain this long therefore runs the loop out of rounds, and
// the point of the fixture is that this is reported rather than hidden -
// `optimizer.py`'s report gains a `Note` line and the `--json` payload's
// `stats.converged` is false (see run_tests.check_optimizer_cap_note).
//
// The quads left behind are the ones the unoptimized program would have run
// anyway, so the differential `-O` check on this file still passes: a capped
// run is a less-reduced program, never a wrong one.
//
//     python interpreter.py tests/optimizer_deep_chain.src       prints 1
//     python interpreter.py tests/optimizer_deep_chain.src -O    prints 1
//     python optimizer.py   tests/optimizer_deep_chain.src       reports the cap

// Every a(k) is written and then read only by a(k+1); nothing reads a14, so
// the whole chain is dead. `n` is a parameter, so constant propagation cannot
// fold any of it away first and leave nothing for this fixture to test.
int deep(int n) {
    var int a1, a2, a3, a4, a5, a6, a7, a8, a9, a10, a11, a12, a13, a14;
    a1 = n + n;
    a2 = a1 + n;
    a3 = a2 + n;
    a4 = a3 + n;
    a5 = a4 + n;
    a6 = a5 + n;
    a7 = a6 + n;
    a8 = a7 + n;
    a9 = a8 + n;
    a10 = a9 + n;
    a11 = a10 + n;
    a12 = a11 + n;
    a13 = a12 + n;
    a14 = a13 + n;
    return n;
}

void main() {
    print(deep(1));
}
